  "worked_solution": "To determine the intervals of continuity of a function, we take the domain of the function and exclude any points of discontinuity.\n\nThe function is defined on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(-1,\\infty ).$ There is a discontinuity at $x=-1,$ but this is already excluded.\n\nTherefore, the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(-1,\\infty ).$  It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nAmong the answer choices, the correct one is the following:\n\n$\\,$ $(-5,-1)$ \n\nNotice that the intervals $(-3,\\infty ),$  $(-3,5),$  $(-\\infty ,0)$  and $(-3,10)$ contain the excluded point $x=-1,$ so the function is not continuous on any of these  these intervals.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
